## Issues

### Bloat findings (this brevity sweep)

- [ ] `outcomes.desired` has 8 items (cap 4). Items 5-7 are implementation
  contracts: launcher shipping mechanism with the exact path
  (`.aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs`), exact TUI tree-vs-panel width percentages
  (55-60%), specific frontmatter-card structure with named sub-elements,
  detail-panel rendering rules per file type. Compress to 3-4 domain
  success criteria — what the human exploring the tree actually needs.
  Traces to: `outcomes.desired` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (file paths, exact percentages, layout details)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `outcomes.undesired` has 8 items, paragraph length, encoding very
  specific failure modes (frontmatter-as-raw-YAML, tree-divergence-from-discover,
  full-screen-drill-in-hides-tree, plan-and-todo-rendered-with-intent-template).
  Cap is 3-4 items ≤2 sentences. Trim to the falsifiable domain failures:
  divergence from `aide_discover`, static-vs-interactive, layout-loss-during-drill-in.
  Traces to: `outcomes.undesired` | Brevity Contract caps
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity

- [ ] `## Strategy` has 9 paragraph-length decisions (cap ~5 ≤80 words each).
  Half encode component-tree details — Ink component hierarchy, "scan and classify
  utilities reused as library code", "buildTree formatter is not reused", flat
  hierarchy with named components. These are plan-level / code-level decisions.
  Traces to: `## Strategy` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (function/symbol names)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Brevity, Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Good examples` reproduces three full ASCII-art terminal panel mockups
  with exact box-drawing characters, exact column widths, exact tree node
  text, exact footer hint strings ("[↑↓] navigate", "[enter] drill in",
  "[tab] deep view"). Per the contract, "exact strings the implementation
  produces" are forbidden, and examples should be 2-3 brief pattern-illustrative
  scenarios, not implementation specifications.
  Traces to: `## Good examples` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content (exact strings, format tokens)
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] `## Bad examples` includes a full-screen mockup, a flat list mockup, and
  raw YAML — all carrying the same exact-string content as Good. Compress
  to 2-3 bad-pattern descriptions without reproducing the bytes.
  Traces to: `## Bad examples` | Brevity Contract caps + Forbidden content
  Misalignment: spec-bloat
  Category: Forbidden-content

- [ ] Sibling redundancy with `src/tools/discover/.aide`: this spec commits
  to "the tree renders the same information aide_discover returns —
  descriptions, alignment status flags, file types, and ancestor chain
  context" — which is the discover spec's own contract. Push the
  scan-and-classify and aide_discover-parity invariants up to the
  trunk (`src/.aide`) or to a shared utility parent rather than restating
  them on both sides.
  Traces to: outcomes, Strategy | Sibling-redundancy doctrine
  Misalignment: spec-gap
  Category: Sibling-redundancy

### Carry-forward (prior implementation drift, may still be open)

The prior todo carries 12 implementation-drift / test-gap items from 5 rounds
of TUI build-fix loops. Re-verify each against current code after the spec
rewrite — some may resolve naturally because the rewritten spec drops
implementation-detail commitments the implementation was trying to honor.
Items most likely to remain real after the rewrite:
- Plan parser format mismatch (real plan files use `### Phase N — Title` and
  embedded `[x]`; parser regex mismatches both)
- aide_discover description-field parity (TUI shows `description`-only, while
  aide_discover falls back to first sentence of `intent`)

These are real implementation-drift findings, not spec drift; rewrite the
spec first, then re-run QA against the rewritten spec.

## Retro

The TUI spec was authored by a strategist who tried to over-specify the
terminal layout to lock in a precise visual contract. The result is a spec
where Strategy and Examples both encode implementation details that should
have lived in the plan: exact column widths, exact box characters, exact
key bindings. Each round of implementation drift produced a new "fix the
implementation to match the mockup" QA loop instead of "fix the spec to
stop encoding rendering details."

The mockup-as-contract pattern is the bloat anti-pattern this spec needs
to retire. The spec should commit to "two-panel layout, tree on left
remains active during drill-in, frontmatter renders as a structured card
not raw YAML" without naming column widths or box characters. Rendering
details live in code with screenshot tests if anything specific must be
locked.
